{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"SCOPe (release 2.08, stable) places all three in one superfamily, a.25.1 Ferritin-like, inside fold a.25 (Ferritin-like) and class a (All alpha proteins). Within that superfamily they sit in three different families: ferritin in a.25.1.1 (Ferritin), ribonucleotide reductase R2-like proteins in a.25.1.2 (Ribonucleotide reductase-like; the R2 protein node is sunid 47257 under family 47253), and manganese catalase in a.25.1.3 (Manganese catalase, T-catalase; family sunid 100951). The superfamily-level homology record for a.25.1 has homology_implied_by_level=true with homology_scope=sf and no warnings, so SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry for the three at the superfamily level. The superfamily annotation comment says the fold 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle'. That is the only shared architectural feature SCOPe records, and it is a structural comment, not a functional one.

Interpretation: the chemistry differences are real but they are at the family and protein level, and SCOPe does not record them. From pretrained knowledge (not verified in SCOPe), ferritin oxidises Fe(II) at a ferroxidase centre and stores iron in a mineral core, R2 subunits carry a diiron-tyrosyl radical cofactor used by the R1 subunit for nucleotide reduction, and Mn catalase uses a dimanganese site to dismute H2O2. A superfamily-level homology call is compatible with these divergent chemistries: a shared four-helix-bundle, bimetal-centre architecture can be inherited while metal identity, ligand set, and reaction diverge. The correct reading is 'probable common ancestry of the ferritin-like bimetal-bundle superfamily, with divergent functions across three families'. It is not 'the same enzyme activity', and it is not 'analogous or convergent'. SCOPe makes no negative claim about these families, and its family separation is the stronger signal that ferritin's iron-storage role must not be transferred to R2 or to Mn catalase.

Limits and unsafe transfers: (1) SCOPe gives no metal identity, substrate, reaction, or cofactor for any family in this superfamily; the 'complexed with mg' comment on d1r03a_ (human mitochondrial ferritin, species node 109785) and the 'complexed with ca, mn3, oh' comment on d1jkua_ (Mn catalase from Lactobacillus plantarum, species node 74707) are crystallographic ligand notes, not functional annotations. (2) Representative choice matters: the Mn catalase and ferritin domains retrieved are single representatives; no R2 domain SID was retrieved, so R2 placement rests on the family node and the protein node 47257, not on a domain lineage. (3) Name searches are misleading here. 'Ribonucleotide reductase' also returns R1 subunit domains in a.98.1 and c.7.1, which are unrelated to the R2 family. 'Catalase' returns a heme catalase-named record (d2caea_, sccs e.5.1.1), which is not in a.25.1. (4) I did not retrieve BLAST or FATCAT hits, aligned residue ranges, or any cross-family structural comparisons, so I cannot say how much sequence or structural similarity these families share, or whether any hit supports a finer relationship than superfamily. (5) I used stable release 2.08 and did not query the periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16 (shown in service info) or any historical release. Any claim about a reclassification would need those releases.","evidence_used":["Read: /tmp/scop-claude-pretrained_mcp_skill-Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence-6bdioutv/workspace/scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/a.25.1/parents"